From 37de825409bc2817b7d2ef84548f2ef2c4ac8526 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 07:23:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 070/119] scene-referred S2: one light-unit convention -
 radiometric lights, 1/PI in the BRDF

Second item of Stage S2, and the one that changes what a scene looks like. CRYENGINE omits the
1/PI from BOTH of its lobes - the Burley diffuse (albedo/PI * scatterL * scatterV) and the GGX
NDF (alpha^2 / (PI * denom^2)) - and pays for it by pre-dividing the sun and the skybox on the
light side, while local lights are left alone. The sun is correct today, every point light is
1.65 stops hot relative to it, and four separate places undo the divide by hand. This item makes
the scene-referred path use one convention: every light radiometric, the 1/PI where the physics
has it.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s2-pi-unification.md, approach A. Research:
scene-notes/research/s2-pi-and-light-units.md.

- ConvertIlluminanceToLightColor() takes the divide as a PARAMETER instead of branching on the
  switch, which is a deliberate refinement of the decision note's one-line summary and the one
  place this item departs from it. The two call sites mean two different things by that divide,
  and the research says so at s2-pi-and-light-units.md section 1.2 and
  s2-skybox-emittance-collision.md section 4.1: for the SUN it is a BRDF compensation and must
  move with the 1/PI; for the SKYBOX it is the E = PI * L conversion of a uniform hemisphere,
  which is what makes the authored "Intensity (lux)" mean the number a light meter pointed at
  the ground would read. Making the helper itself branch would have silently multiplied the sky
  by PI and made the preset in item 5 lie about its own units. The skybox therefore keeps its
  divide on both paths; its real unit error is the times-ten at Renderer.cpp:4007, which is
  item 3. The decision note has been corrected.

- All four manual undos are gated. Three are renderer-side (D3DHWShader.cpp's two volumetric-fog
  scattering colours and VolumetricFog.cpp's sun constant) and one is the forward replication in
  TimeOfDay.cpp that recomputes the sun for the volumetric clouds - ungated in stock CE, missed
  by the audit, and the one that desyncs the clouds from the sun by PI if it moves alone.
  r_VolumetricFogSunLightCorrection keeps its full meaning and its content-compatibility role;
  only the body is skipped.

- The renderer undos ask CRendererResources::IsSceneReferredLightUnits(), a NEW gate that reads
  E3DPARAM_SCENE_REFERRED and not the post-effect bus. That is not a shortcut: the sun colour
  they are correcting was computed under the engine-side mirror and is cached there until the
  next time-of-day update, so the correction has to be on the same clock as the number. Asking
  the bus would mean asking a different clock and getting a PI-wrong frame on every flip.

- The BRDF factor is ONE per-view constant, CV_SceneExposure.z, stored minus one exactly like
  the exposure in .x so that an unbound constant buffer and the stock path both read exactly
  1.0. It reaches the shaders as SCENE_BRDF_NORM_FACTOR and lands in the two places that were
  already multiplying by a constant at that point: SpecularBRDF's NormalizationFactor (which the
  5-argument overload was passing a literal 1.0f to) and BurleyBRDF's energyFactor. Cost: zero
  ALU, ZERO new permutations. A static flag would have doubled Illum, Terrain, HumanSkin, Glass,
  MultiLayeredMaterials, Vegetation and TiledShading to save nothing.

- The legacy .tod migration loses its times-PI on the scene-referred path. The multiply there is
  not part of the file-format conversion - it is the inverse of the divide that
  ConvertIlluminanceToLightColor is about to apply - so with no divide there must be no
  multiply. BEHAVIOUR CHOSEN, and it is path-dependent by nature: the migration runs at LOAD
  time, normally before any camera has activated, so a pre-3.8.2 .tod loaded in a stock session
  and then viewed on the ReC path reads PI times too bright. Gating it is still right (the
  alternative is wrong on both paths, and Editor/asset_thumbnail.tod would go PI-squared wrong),
  and the remedy - re-save the preset with the camera active, or author a .env - goes in item
  5's docs.

Deliberately untouched, per the decision's guard list: ambient lights (DLF_AMBIENT, a
multiplicative tint, and the TiledLightVolumes.cpp:893 / TiledShading.cfi:840 guards already
separate them), environment probes (a cosine-weighted cubemap is already E/PI and never enters a
BRDF), emissive materials (radiance by definition), and the times-121 / divided-by-121 bulb round
trip with its CPU twin GetIntensityScale() - folding PI into that would desync culling radii from
shading. None of the three call DiffuseBRDF or SpecularBRDF, which is what makes the single
constant safe.

Known breakage carried forward, not hidden (D13): area lights go through AreaLightLTC_GGX, which
never calls SpecularBRDF, so they do NOT receive the 1/PI and are now 1.65 stops hot relative to
point lights on this path; research section 6 question 1 (whether LTCTex_2.w already carries the
missing 1/(2PI)) has to be measured before that is corrected. OrenNayarBRDF is defined but called
by nothing, so it needs no gate today.

Content consequence, which is not gateable and is the point of D1: on the scene-referred path
every existing point, projector and area light reads 1.65 stops darker and the sun is unchanged,
so any level opened with the camera active looks different by design. Documented in item 5.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): Cry3DEngine, CryRenderD3D11,
CinematicCamera.

